## ABSTRACT
Heterogeneous materials made of metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) and optically active nanomaterials have attracted intensive interests in recent years due to their distinct physicochemical properties, but controllable fabrication of these materials remains challenging yet. In this work, we report a new strategy to in situ fabricate heterogeneous nanomaterials based on \(\mathsf{U i O-66-N H}_{2}\) and upconversion nanorods (UCNRs) via a hierarchical and dynamic assembly process. Core–satellite structured UiO-66- \(\mathsf{N H}_{2}@\) UCNRs have been successfully fabricated, and the formation mechanism was thoroughly investigated by the combined use of scanning electron microscopy (SEM) and Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) spectroscopy. Our results revealed the involvement of three main stages: supramolecular assembly of \(\mathsf{U i O-66-N H}_{2}\) precursors with UCNRs, nucleation and growth of \(\mathsf{U i O-66-N H}_{2}\) crystal, and dynamic assembly with UCNRs accompanied by Ostwald ripening. Furthermore, based on the hereditary optical and porous features of the heterogeneous nanomaterials, an enhanced multimodal synergistic anticancer platform has been established by integrating near-infrared (NIR)-triggered photodynamic therapy (PDT) and pH-triggered anticancer drug delivery, as confirmed by cellular experiments. The present study provides a new avenue for developing advanced functional heterogeneous nanomaterials via the hierarchical and dynamic assembly strategy.
## KEYWORDS
heterogeneous nanomaterials, metal-organic frameworks, upconversion nanorods, assembly, therapy
## 1 Introduction
In recent years, with the continuing development and progress of nanotechnology, heterogeneous nanomaterials, constructed by two nanoparticles of unique nature [1], have been created as an important class of composite nanostructures for energy storage [2], sensing [3], catalysis [4, 5], and cancer therapy [6, 7]. One prominent characteristic of these heterogeneous nanomaterials is their exquisitely multifunctional properties due to cooperative functions, which are greater than the physical mixtures of all components [8]. Therefore, many types of heterogeneous nanomaterials have been designed and fabricated to achieve enhanced and synergistic performances in the past years [9–12].
Among various types of building components, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), synthesized by self-assembly of metal ions/clusters and organic ligands through coordination bonds, have emerged as powerful platforms for many applications, attributing to their tunable structural features and pore sizes, high surface areas, good biocompatibility, and versatile functionality [13–18]. Consequently, heterogeneous nanomaterials based on MOFs have attracted more and more research interests in recent years [19–23]. In fact, combining MOFs with other nanomaterials can not only surmount the inherent limitations of individual materials, but also endow MOFs with new functions [1, 8, 24]. Particularly, composites of MOF with optically active nanorods exhibit distinct optical properties, which makes them attractive for applications in the fields of anticounterfeiting, optogenetics, biosensing, therapeutics, etc. [25–28]. It is noteworthy that although MOF-based heterogeneous nanomaterials have made great progress, the reported approaches still encounter some limitations and challenges for inlaying anistropic nanorods with MOFs in a simply and efficient manner [29, 30]. The typical method usually requires pretreatment or modification of nanorods to avoid the randomly agglomerated or nonuniform morphology [1, 31]. Moreover, these processes may also cause fluorescence-quenching and charge/energy transfer inhibition, which are unfavorable for their further functionalization and application [32, 33]. Therefore, there is an urgent need to develop new fabrication strategy to circumvent these restrictions for further advancing the applications of these MOF-nanorod composites.
