Herein, we report a new approach to facilely prepare heterogeneous nanomaterials of MOF and upconversion nanorods (UCNRs) via in situ hierarchical and dynamic assembly without any pretreatment and modification of UCNRs (Scheme 1). The fabricated UiO-66-NH2@UCNRs display interesting core–satellite structures with UCNRs evenly anchored on the outer surface of MOFs, and successfully inherit the outstanding performance of both components. Benefiting from the luminescence and porous properties, these UiO-66-NH2@UCNRs composites were further applied for near-infrared
(NIR)-triggered photodynamic therapy (PDT) via energy transfer from UCNRs to the photosensitizer (TCPP), and pH-triggered anticancer drug (5-fluorouracil, Fu) delivery with negligible premature release. Cellular experiments demonstrated enhanced multimodal synergistic anticancer effect of these core–satellite structured MOF@UCNRs composites, which hold great promise for cancer theranostics (Scheme 1).
## 2 Experimental section
## 2.1 Synthesis of UiO-66-NH\(_2\)@UCNRs
Firstly, the as-synthesized oleic acid (OA, \(\mathrm{C_{18}H_{34}O_{2}}\))-capped UCNRs \((100\ \ \upmu\mathrm{L})\), dispersed in cyclohexane \(\left(\mathrm{C}_{6}\mathrm{H}_{12}\right)\), were precipitated by adding \(500~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) ethanol \(\left(\mathrm{C}_{21}\mathrm{H}_{8}\mathrm{O}\right)\), and collected by centrifugation at \(9{,}000\ \mathrm{rpm}\) for \(5~\mathrm{min}\). The precipitated UCNRs were washed with ethanol for several times and dispersed in \(5\;\mathrm{mL}\) N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF). Subsequently, \(1.13~\mathrm{mL}\) of glacial acetic acid \(\left(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COOH}\right)\), \(98\%\) and \(10~\mathrm{~mL}\) DMF solution containing 2-aminoterephthalic acid \(\left(\mathrm{BDC-NH}_{2}\right.\), \(4.62\,\mathrm{~mg})\), and \(\mathrm{ZrCl}_{4}\) \(\left(6.3\;\mathrm{mg}\right)\) were mixed with \(5~\mathrm{mL}\) of UCNRs in DMF. Then, the mixture solution was treated with ultrasound for \(10\;\mathrm{min}\) and heated to \(120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(24~\mathrm{h}\) to obtain UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\) UCNRs. The final products were collected by centrifugation at \(8{,}000\ \mathrm{rpm}\) for \(5~\mathrm{\min}\), and washed three times with DMF and ethanol, respectively.
In order to explore the formation mechanism of UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\) UCNRs, \(500~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of the reaction mixture was extracted from the reaction system at different time intervals. The extracted products were collected by centrifugation at \(8{,}000\ \mathrm{rpm}\) for \(5~\mathrm{min}\), washed by DMF for three times, and finally dispersed in methanol \(\left(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\mathrm{O}\right)\) for further measurement.
## 2.2 Synthesis of UiO-66-NH\(_2\)@UCNRs-TCPP
\(2~\mathrm{mL}\) ethanol solution, containing 1-(3-dimethylaminopropyl)-3- ethylcarbodiimide hydrochloride \((4.8\mathrm{~\ensuremath~{~\mg~}~}\) and Nhydroxysuccinimide \((2.8~\mathrm{mg})\), was mixed with \(0.5~\mathrm{mg}\) TCPP in \(1~\mathrm{mL}\) ethanol solution. The mixture was stirred for \(1\;\mathrm{{h}}\) at room temperature. Then, \(5~\mathrm{~mL}\) ethanol solution of UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\) UCNRs \((1.56~\mathrm{mg})\) was gradually added into the mixture, followed by heating at \(65~^{\circ}\!C\) for \(3\,\mathrm{~h~}\). UiO-66- \(\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\) UCNRsTCPP was obtained by centrifugation, and then washed three times with ethanol.
## 2.3 Fu loading and release
The production of UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\) UCNRs-TCPP was immersed in saturated aqueous solution of Fu for \(24~\mathrm{{h}}\) at room temperature. The Fu-loaded UiO-66- \(\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\) UCNRs-TCPP was collected by centrifugation, and washed twice with deionized \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\). The loading efficiency of Fu was calculated by the following equation: loading efficiency \(=\ M_{\mathrm{Loading}}/M_{\mathrm{Total}}\ \times\ 100\%\). In the equation, \(M_{\mathrm{Loading}}\) represents the maximum amount of Fu released from the composite at \(\mathrm{pH}=5.4\), and \(M_{\mathrm{Total}}\) represents the total content of Fu-loaded UiO-66- \(\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\) UCNRs-TCPP.
The drug release assays of Fu-loaded UiO-66- \(\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\) UCNRSTCPP \(\left(1\;\mathrm{mg}\right)\) were performed in phosphate buffer solution (PBS) of different \(\mathrm{pH}\) (5.4, 6.4, or 7.4). Briefly, \(100~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) aqueous solution of Fu-loaded UiO-66- \(\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\) UCNRs-TCPP \(\left(1~\mathrm{mg}\right)\) was placed in a dialysis bag \((M_{\mathrm{W}};2,000\;\mathrm{Da})\), then it was transferred to a cuvette containing \(3\ \mathrm{mL}\) PBS and gently stirred. The absorbance of the solution at different time was recorded by ultraviolet–visible (UV–vis) absorption spectroscopy to quantify the released amount of Fu. Finally, the \(\mathrm{Fu@UiO-66-NH_{2}@U C N R s-T C P P}\) placed in PBS solution was stirred for 1 day to achieve complete drug release.
## 2.4 Confocal laser scanning microscopy (CLSM)
\(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH_{2}@U C N R s-T C P P}\) was added into HeLa cells which were grown on glass-bottomed dishes overnight at \(37~^{\circ}\!C\) and \(5\%\) \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\). After incubation for \(4\,\mathrm{{h}}\), the medium was removed, and cells were gently washed with PBS buffer \((\mathrm{pH}=7.4)\) for three times to remove noninternalized materials. Next, the cell membrane was stained with CellMask™green plasma membrane stain for \(15~\mathrm{min}\), and cells were observed using a CLSM. The fluorescence of UiO-66- \(\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) was recorded in the range of \(440{-}480\ \mathrm{nm}\) (green channel) with \(405~\mathrm{~nm}\) excitation, and the fluorescence of membrane tracker was detected in a spectral window from 530 to \(570\,\mathrm{nm}\) (red channel) with \(488\,\mathrm{nm}\) excitation.
## 2.5 Extracellular and intracellular reactive oxygen species (ROS) detection
The generation of extracellular ROS was measured with singlet oxygen sensor green (SOSG) probe. Typically, aqueous solution of different materials was mixed with \(5~\upmu\mathrm{M}\) SOSG aqueous solution. Then, the samples were irradiated with \(980\,\mathrm{nm}\) laser light for 0, 5, 10, 15, and \(20\,\mathrm{min}\). Every \(5\,\mathrm{min}\), the fluorescence intensity
of SOSG was recorded on a fluorescence spectrophotometer \((\lambda_{\mathrm{ex}}=504\;\mathrm{nm}\) and \(\lambda_{\mathrm{em}}=525\,\mathrm{nm})\).
The generation of intracellular ROS was measured via CLSM using 2,7-dichlorofluorescein diacetate (DCFH-DA) probe \((\lambda_{\mathrm{ex}}=488\ \mathrm{nm}\), monitored in the range of \(500{-}550\ \mathrm{nm})\). After treating HeLa cells with UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\) UCNRs-TCPP \((50\,\upmu\mathrm{g}\cdot\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\) and \(4\,\mathrm{~h~})\), the medium was removed. Afterwards, DCFH-DA \((10\,\upmu\mathrm{M})\) was added and incubated for \(20\;\mathrm{min}\) before washed three times with PBS \((\mathrm{pH}=7.4)\). Thereafter, cells were further incubated with or without a \(980\ \mathrm{nm}\) laser light \((1\ \mathrm{W}\cdot\mathrm{cm}^{-2})\) irradiation for \(10\ \mathrm{min}\). Then the cells were imaged by CLSM with excitation at \(488\,\mathrm{nm}\).
## 2.6 Cytotoxicity assay
HeLa cells were incubated in 96-well plate at a density of \(1\ \times\ 10^{4}\) cells/well for \(24\,\mathrm{~h~}\). Then the cells were treated with increasing concentrations \((0{-}200\ \ \ \upmu\mathrm{g}{\cdot}\mathrm{mL}^{-1})\) of UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\) UCNRs-TCPP or \(\mathrm{Fu@UiO-66-NH_{2}@}\) UCNRs-TCPP at \(37\ \ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). Then the culture medium was removed and fresh medium was added. After irradiation with or without \(980\ \mathrm{nm}\) NIR laser for \(10\;\mathrm{min}\) \((1\ \mathrm{W}\cdot\mathrm{cm}^{-2})\), cells were incubated for another \(24\ \mathrm{h}\). Finally, \(10\%\) CCK-8 solution was added and cultured for \(15\,\mathrm{min}\) to evaluate the cytotoxicity by the microplate reader.
## 3 Results and discussion
## 3.1 Synthesis and formation mechanism of UiO-66- \(\mathbf{NH}_{2}@\) UCNRs
In order to fabricate heterogeneous nanorods-decorated MOFs, we first synthesized UCNRs. The \(\mathrm{NaYF_{4}:Y b/T m}\) core of UCNRs with an average diameter of \(23.1\ \mathrm{nm}\) (Fig. 1(a) and Fig. S1(a) in
Then \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH}_{2}\) was chosen as the matrix material to synthesize the heterogeneous MOFs with nanorods. OA-capped UCNRs were directly mixed with \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH}_{2}\) precursors and reacted at \(120\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(24\ \mathrm{h}\). The PXRD patterns (Fig. S5 in the ESM) confirm that the heterogeneous nanomaterials remain the crystal structures of \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH}_{2}\) and UCNRs. Scanning electron microscopy (SEM, Fig. 1(c)) and TEM (Fig. 1(d) and Fig. S6 in the
ESM) images reveal that the resulted heterogeneous nanomaterials possess octahedral geometry and uniformly distributed UCNRs on the surface. As shown in Fig. S7 in the ESM, the addition of OA-capped UCNRs did not affect the morphology of \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) during the preparation of heterogeneous nanomaterials. Energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS) (Fig. S8 in the ESM) and elemental mapping images (Figs. 1(e)–1(i)) display the distribution of elements in a single heterogeneous nanoparticle. The presence of primary elements of UCNRs and \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2},\) i.e., F, O, Yb, Y, and \(Z\mathbf{r},\) further proved the efficient integration of UCNRs with \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) . In contrast, only few UCNRs were observed in the center of the heterogeneous nanoparticle (Figs. 1(e), 1(g), and 1(h)), which indicates the inhomogeneous distribution of UCNRs in the nanocomposites. In order to acquire the exact distribution of UCNRs, the frozen section TEM technique was employed. The results showed that UCNRs only paved on the surface of \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2},\) and no UCNRs existed inside the \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) crystals (Fig. S9 in the ESM). As confirmed by dynamic light scattering (DLS), the mean diameter of \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) is about \(250\,\textrm{n m}\) , and it increases to \(383~\mathrm{nm}\) for the surface decorated \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\) UCNRs (Fig. S10 in the ESM). Furthermore, the stability of \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\) UCNRs in PBS at various \(\mathrm{pH}\) values was examined. Both SEM results (Fig. S11 in the ESM) and PXRD results (Fig. S12 in the ESM) indicated that the crystallinity and structural integrity of UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\) UCNRs could be preserved in PBS solution for two days \((\mathrm{pH}=7.4{-}5.4)\) .
To explore the formation mechanism of the UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\) UCNRs, we first extracted the reaction mixture at different time intervals, which was characterized by SEM and Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) spectroscopy. Within \(60\ \mathrm{min}\) , UCNRs showed a trend of gradual assembly in a plane with \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2},\) precursors as the reaction progressed (Figs. 2(a)–2(c)). Meanwhile, the OA on the surface of UCNRs fell off stepwise, which was evidenced by FTIR spectroscopy (Fig. 2(g)) with the disappearance of peaks of \(\mathrm{-CH}_{2}\) (2,926 and \(2{,}853~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}\) ) and \(-C{=}O\) (1,560 and
\(1,466~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}\) ). At a reaction time of \(90\ \mathrm{min}\) , nucleation of UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) occurred, and irregular and heterogeneous assemblies of UCNRs with \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) crystal nucleus appeared (Fig. 2(d)). As time progressed, irregular and heterogeneous assemblies evolved into heterogeneous nanoparticles with polyhedron shape at \(120\ \mathrm{min}\) and grew up gradually (Figs. 2(e) and 2(f)), indicating the growth of \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) . The appearance of new peaks from 1,200 to \(1,800~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}\) in FTIR spectroscopy also indicates that UiO\(66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) was formed (Fig. 2(g)). Finally, \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) grew into octahedral nanoparticles with uniform size and UCNRs were paved on the surface (Fig. 1(c) and Fig. S9 in the ESM).
Based on the above results, we infer that the formation of UiO\(66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\) UCNRs should follow three main stages via hierarchical and dynamic assembly as shown in Fig. 2(h). Firstly, UCNRs assembled with \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) precursors via supramolecular interactions (i.e., van der Waals interactions, electrostatic interactions, and hydrogen bonds) [35–38], which was accompanied by the shedding of OA ligand, gradually inducing UCNRs to become positively charged [8]. Secondly, nucleation of \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) crystal and dynamic assembly with UCNRs occurred as the reaction proceeds. Under the driving force of heat, the nucleation of \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) took place via coordination between 2-aminoterephthalic acid and \(\mathrm{ZrCl}_{4},\) along with the increase of the negativity [8]. Therefore, the plane supramolecular assembly dissociated, and positively charged UCNRs reassembled with negatively charged \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) crystal nucleus under the driving force of the enhanced electrostatic interactions, generating irregular and heterogeneous assemblies. Finally, \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) crystals grew and further assembled with UCNRs to yield UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\) UCNRs. Driven by chelation and heat, the \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) crystal nucleus grew outward, and gradually grew into regular energetically favored octahedron shape as a result of Ostwald ripening [39], generating the most negatively charged surface. Owing to the continually enhanced electrostatic interactions, irregular and heterogeneous assemblies dissociated and UCNRs redeposited onto the surface of energetically favored octahedral
\(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH}_{2}\) crystals, leading to the formation of uniform UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\) UCNRs heterogeneous nanomaterials.
## 3.2 Optical properties and NIR light-triggered PDT
The successful inlay of \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH}_{2}\) with UCNRs provides opportunities to construct novel nanocomposites with unique structures and multifunctionalities. As seen in Fig. 3(a) and Fig. S13 in the ESM, the heterogeneous nanomaterials successfully inherited the typical luminescence behavior of UCNRs and UiO\(66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\), allowing them suitable for further luminescence-based applications. PDT, as a new therapy strategy based on ROS (particularly singlet oxygen \({}^{1}O_{2}\)), has attracted increasing attention in past decades due to its minimal invasion, high spatiotemporal precision, negligible drug resistance, and high selectivity [40–42]. However, the efficacy of PDT in the clinic was limited by the defect of hydrophobic photosensitizers, such as low tissue penetration depth of excitation light in ultraviolet to visible range, complicated transport, and self-quenching caused by agglomeration in water, etc. Noteworthily, the outstanding upconversion luminescence performance and nanostructures of \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH}_{2}@\) UCNRs allow us to design NIR light-triggered PDT via fluorescence resonance energy transfer (FRET) from
UCNRs to the photosensitizers, offering the potential to solve these limitations. In view of the short half-life and effective radius of ROS [43,44], we grafted the porphyrinic photosensitizer TCPP onto the surface of \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH}_{2}@\) UCNRs to guarantee the minimum distance between the photosensitizer and cells. Covalent condensation reaction between the carboxyl group of TCPP and amino group of \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH}_{2}\) was carried out to avoid the loss of the photosensitizer from leakage and diffusion [45,46]. The khaki-colored \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH}_{2}@\) UCNRs turned into deep brown \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH}_{2}@\) UCNRs-TCPP after TCPP decoration (Fig. S14 in the ESM). Based on the molecular size [47], large sized TCPP molecules (ca. \(2\;\mathrm{nm}\)) cannot be captured into the UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) pores (diameter ca. \(1.36\;\mathrm{nm}\)) [43]. Thus, TCPP species are expected to locate on the surface of \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH}_{2}@\) UCNRs. The successful TCPP decoration was confirmed by fluorescence emission spectra. As shown in Fig. 3(b), \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH}_{2}@\) UCNRs-TCPP nanoparticles show strong emission at both 650 and \(715\;\mathrm{nm}\) upon excitation at \(420\;\mathrm{nm}\), which confirms that TCPP molecules are conjugated to the \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH}_{2}@\) UCNRs successfully and the photosensitization property of TCPP is well preserved after surface decoration.
UV–vis absorption spectra were also used to verify the
functionalization of UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\@UCNRs. The typical porphyrin sharp Soret band at \(419\ \mathrm{nm}\) and four weak Q bands (520, 558, 599, and \(652\ \mathrm{nm}\)) also confirmed that UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\@UCNRs-TCPP was formed (Fig. S15 in the ESM). The PXRD patterns and morphology of UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\@UCNRs-TCPP were the same as those of primitive UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\@UCNRs, implying that the structure of UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\@UCNRs remains unchanged after the introduction of porphyrin species (Figs. S5 and S16 in the ESM). In addition, DLS (Fig. S10 in the ESM) showed that the average diameter of UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\@UCNRs-TCPP was slightly increased to \(387\ \mathrm{nm}\).
The UV–vis absorption spectrum of the TCPP overlaps with upconversion luminescence spectrum of UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\@UCNRs over a broad wavelength range (Fig. 3(c)), and the close proximity between TCPP with UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\@UCNRs in UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\@UCNRs-TCPP enables the occurrence of FRET from UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\@UCNRs to TCPP. As shown in Fig. 3(a), compared to UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\@UCNRs, the strong inhibition of the \(\mathrm{Tm}^{3+}\) blue emission bands after forming UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\@UCNRs-TCPP evidenced an efficient FRET between the pair. The increase in the ratio of upconversion luminescence intensity \((I_{800}/I_{450} = 3.3\) and \(I_{800}/I_{475} = 3.0)\) of the UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\@UCNRs-TCPP in comparison with that of UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\@UCNRs \((I_{800}/I_{450} = 0.5\) and \(I_{800}/I_{475} = 1.0)\) could be attributed to the remarkable overlap between the absorption of TCPP and the emission of UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\@UCNRs in the region of \(400{-}500\ \mathrm{nm}\) (Fig. 3(c)).
Due to the energy transferred from UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\@UCNRs to TCPP, it is reasoned that \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) could be produced from the UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\@UCNRs-TCPP nanocomposites with \(980\ \mathrm{nm}\) NIR light irradiation (Fig. 3(e)), well within the tissue transparency window. Accordingly, SOSG was used to assess the production of \({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) from the UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\@UCNRs-TCPP nanocomposites upon \(980\ \mathrm{nm}\) laser excitation. The reagent SOSG reacts with \({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) to generate green fluorescence that can be characterized with fluorescence spectrophotometer. As shown in Fig. 3(d), upon \(980\ \mathrm{nm}\) laser irradiation, a significant increase of the fluorescence of SOSG at \(525\ \mathrm{nm}\) was observed for UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\@UCNRs-TCPP as irradiation time increased, indicating a steady singlet oxygen generation. As a control, UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\@UCNRs and TCPP could not generate \({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) with unobvious fluorescence change under the \(980\ \mathrm{nm}\) laser irradiation.
The cellular uptake of UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\@UCNRs-TCPP nanocomposites by HeLa cells was further investigated by fluorescence imaging. Fluorescence emission from the membrane tracker and UiO-66-NH\(_2\) was collected in two color channels, which are depicted in red and green (Fig. 4(a)), respectively. The bright green luminescence from UiO-66-NH\(_2\) segment in UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\@UCNRs-TCPP ingested by HeLa cells could easily be observed in the cytoplasm (Fig. 4(a)), suggesting that UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\@UCNRs-TCPP nanocomposites could be effectively ingested by HeLa cells. Subsequently, DCFH-DA was used as an indicator to monitor intracellular \({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) generation for different treatment groups. The cells treated with UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\@UCNRs-TCPP under \(980\ \mathrm{nm}\) laser irradiation showed bright green fluorescence, indicating the \({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) generation (Fig. 4(b)). In contrast, no obvious green fluorescence was seen for cells treated with nonirradiated UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\@UCNRs-TCPP.
## 3.3 pH-triggered drug delivery and synergistic anticancer effect
Smart drug delivery and multimodal therapy have attracted increasing attentions due to their potential to realize enhanced anticancer effect [48–50]. The high surface areas and porosity of UiO-66-NH\(_2\) inspire us to further exploit the smart drug delivery
performance of the fabricated UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\@UCNRs-TCPP. In order to deliver anticancer drugs, \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption–desorption analysis was used to measure the surface area of UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\@UCNRs, and UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\@UCNRs-TCPP. As indicated in Figs. S17 and S18 in the ESM, the Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) surface areas of UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\@UCNRs, and UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\@UCNRs-TCPP were calculated to be 712, 515, and \(491\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), respectively. Compared to the pristine UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\@UCNRs, the obvious decline in the surface area for UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\@UCNRs is likely caused by the incorporation of nonporous UCNRs. Meanwhile, only a slight decrease of the surface area for UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\@UCNRs-TCPP was found compared to UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\@UCNRs, which further confirmed that TCPP molecules are located on the surface of UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\@UCNRs, rather than inside the pores. Moreover, the typical type-I adsorption isothermal features and pore size distributions were basically the same as UiO-66-NH\(_2\) (Fig. S19 in the ESM), which is suitable for drug encapsulation [45]. The porous nanocomposites exhibited positive charge in water, thus negatively-charged chemotherapy drugs (i.e., Fu) could be loaded into the pores through electrostatic interactions (Fig. S20 in the ESM) to achieve the final nanocomposites Fu@UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\@UCNRs-TCPP. The loading efficiency of Fu was calculated to be \(6.5\ \mathrm{wt.\%}\) through the UV–vis absorption spectroscopy (Figs. S21 and S22(d) in the ESM). The electrostatic interaction-induced drug encapsulation makes pH-triggered release possible, which could minimize undesired release and command the effective release of the antitumor drug in tumor cells with lower pH level [51]. The Fu release profiles at different pH values were shown in Fig. 5(a) and Fig. S22 in the ESM. Fu molecules could be firmly capped within the nanopores at \(\mathrm{pH} = 7.4\), exhibiting negligible premature release (less than \(5\%\)). When lowering the pH to 6.4, slight release of Fu could be observed, indicating pH can trigger the release process. At a more acidic pH of 5.4, the release rate of Fu became much faster, and over \(70\%\) of Fu was released within \(60\ \mathrm{min}\).
Furthermore, the cytotoxicity of UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\@UCNRs-TCPP before and after the drug encapsulation was investigated by using CCK-8 assay against HeLa cells at various concentrations with or without \(980\ \mathrm{nm}\) laser irradiation for \(10\ \mathrm{min}\). As shown in Fig. 5(b) and Fig. S23 in the ESM, the viability of cells treated with UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\@UCNRs-TCPP remained above \(97\%\) in the absence of light irradiation, indicating that UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\@UCNRs-TCPP possessed excellent biocompatibility and negligible dark cytotoxicity. Fu@UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\@UCNRs-TCPP showed high dark cytotoxicity, and the cell viability decreased up to \(50\%\), indicating the pH-triggered release of antitumor drug Fu in HeLa cells. Particularly, the toxicity of UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\@UCNRs-TCPP was observed upon NIR irradiations, resulting more than \(60\%\) cell death due to the \({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) generation. Notably, incubation of HeLa cells with Fu@UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\@UCNRs-TCPP following NIR irradiation led to even higher cytotoxicity (up to \(90\%\) cell death) than that of the cells treated with either Fu@UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\@UCNRs-TCPP without irradiation or UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\@UCNRs-TCPP with irradiation, indicating an enhanced anticancer efficiency could be achieved through the combination of chemotherapy and NIR-induced PDT.
## 4 Conclusions
In summary, we have developed a new strategy to fabricate MOF-based heterogeneous nanomaterials with surface-anchored UCNRs via hierarchical and dynamic assembly. The formation mechanism of these UiO-66-NH\(_2\)\@UCNRs heterogeneous nanocomposites follows three main stages: supramolecular
assembly of \( \mathrm{UiO-66-NH}_{2} \) precursors with UCNRs, nucleation and growth of \( \mathrm{UiO-66-NH}_{2} \) crystal, and dynamic assembly with UCNRs accompanied by Ostwald ripening. The heterogeneous nanocomposites successfully inherited the merits of both components, including distinct luminescence, high surface area, and porous structure. Consequently, NIR-triggered PDT platform has been established based on energy transfer from UCNRs to
TCPP for potential anticancer applications. Upon further encapsulating anti-cancer drugs Fu into the pores via electrostatic interactions, \( \mathrm{Fu@UiO-66-NH}_{2} @ \mathrm{UCNRs} \) exhibit excellent pH-triggered release and enhanced multimodal synergistic anticancer effect by combining NIR-induced PDT with chemotherapy. The present strategy of fabricating heterogeneous nanomaterials via hierarchical and dynamic assembly is simple and controllable,
which can be also extended to the development of other functional composite materials.
